  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved comprehensive programmes to facilitate employment for just transitions in the environmental and digital dimensions in urban and rural areas, targeting vulnerable workers, by the end of 2025</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2023, external pressures like conflicts in Eastern Europe and the Gaza Strip, alongside inflation from the COVID-19 pandemic and retail crisis, intensified. Brazil&apos;s new government struggled to advance its agenda in a Congress dominated by opposition, hindering economic and social policies.

Brazil&#x2019;s economic recovery showed progress with a 2.9% GDP growth, record employment of 101 million, and a decline in unemployment to 7.4%. However, informality and low wages persisted, with 39.5 million informal workers and an average real wage of R$3,134. Issues like child labor and high youth unemployment highlighted the need for labor rights and social justice.

The ILO Office for Brazil plans to collaborate with local entities over the next two years to promote labor rights and achieve significant outcomes, including actions for youth employment, enhancing the learning system, increasing union representation, extending social protection to app-based workers, generating knowledge on green jobs, and implementing the Ecological Transformation Plan and National REDD+ Strategy.</narrative>
  </description>
